# AUR packages, pinned by commit.
#
#   <repo>  <splits>  <commit>
#
# repo    the git repository to clone. For a split package this is the
#         package base, not the individual package: AUR keeps the per-package
#         repositories of a split empty and puts the real PKGBUILD in the base.
# splits  comma-separated splits to install, or "-" for a package that is not
#         split. makepkg has no option to build one split of a package base, so
#         every split is built and only the ones named here are installed.
# commit  the exact tree to build. AUR repositories are mutable and their
#         PKGBUILDs run arbitrary code during the build, so the code that goes
#         into an image has to be named here rather than tracked from a branch.
#
# Refresh with:
#
#   while read -r repo splits _; do
#       [[ "$repo" == \#* || -z "$repo" ]] && continue
#       printf '%s %s %s\n' "$repo" "$splits" \
#           "$(git ls-remote "https://aur.archlinux.org/$repo.git" HEAD | cut -f1)"
#   done < build_files/config/02-aur-pkgs
#
# Caveat: a package whose name ends in -git tracks an upstream git repository
# that the PKGBUILD fetches at build time. Pinning the AUR commit pins the
# recipe, not the upstream source, so those are not fully reproducible.
#
# Caveat: the packages listed here are pinned exactly, but when one of them
# depends on another AUR package that is *not* on this list, that dependency is
# resolved by yay at its branch head. Keep such dependencies on this list
# wherever possible so they are pinned too.

# repo                  splits                            commit
# distroshelf has been dropped from the AUR database, but its git
# repository is still reachable, so this pin still builds. It is unmaintained
# and the repository will go away at some point, which will fail the build
# with a clear 'not reachable' error rather than silently building something
# else.
